WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.835
Module 4: story collection.

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:07.409
In the Mosaic course you learned where
prompts come from and what respondents see.

00:00:07.409 --> 00:00:10.468
Here you'll judge prompts
before they go out,

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:13.188
keep every domain supplied with stories,

00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:17.130
and get the link to people who
never read all-staff email.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:22.840
For designing story questions from scratch,
see the Mosaic Signal Specialist course.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.268
About thirty minutes.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.080
The method is sequential on purpose.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.412
The seven teams dig first, and what they
find shapes the questions everyone else is

00:00:32.412 --> 00:00:32.798
asked.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:35.817
That's what makes the
stories worth collecting.

00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:40.186
If a team finds a pattern and hundreds
of stories lean the same way,

00:00:40.186 --> 00:00:42.435
you have a finding worth acting on.

00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:47.189
If the stories disagree, the problem
is real somewhere and not everywhere,

00:00:47.189 --> 00:00:49.181
and now you know where to look.

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:53.100
So draft prompts once the teams
have something to draft from.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.248
Northwind drafted after
eleven days of fieldwork,

00:00:56.248 --> 00:00:58.883
and opened the link the following Monday.

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.084
Every prompt should pass five tests,

00:01:02.084 --> 00:01:06.083
the same rules Culture Dig
gives its own drafting AI.

00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:10.385
One: it asks for one thing
that happened, not an opinion.

00:01:10.385 --> 00:01:13.555
Two: anyone, in any role, could answer it.

00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:18.536
Three: it's genuinely open, so a
good, bad or mixed story all fit.

00:01:18.536 --> 00:01:21.705
Ask about a time collaboration broke down,

00:01:21.705 --> 00:01:25.253
and broken collaboration
is all you'll collect.

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:28.271
Four: it never states a finding as fact.

00:01:28.271 --> 00:01:32.724
"Given that leadership ignores
feedback" leads the witness.

00:01:32.724 --> 00:01:34.988
And five: it points at nobody.

00:01:34.988 --> 00:01:39.969
No specific person, team, incident
or document from the fieldwork.

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:46.082
The AI is told this too, but only you know
the organization well enough to check.

00:01:46.082 --> 00:01:52.044
A prompt that mentions a specific incident
tells everyone what someone told the

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:53.101
research team.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.947
Here are five prompts from Northwind's
review on April the twenty-third.

00:01:58.947 --> 00:02:03.254
Each draft has Edit, Approve,
and a bin icon to delete it.

00:02:03.254 --> 00:02:05.110
There's no reject button.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:09.269
For each, decide: approve,
edit then approve, or delete.

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:12.016
And name the test it passes or fails.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.623
Here's how prompts reach people.

00:02:14.623 --> 00:02:19.865
Each respondent gets one approved prompt,
picked at random from the whole pool,

00:02:19.865 --> 00:02:23.050
across all prompts rather
than domain by domain.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.566
If it doesn't fit, they can
try a different question.

00:02:26.566 --> 00:02:29.419
After they submit,
they're offered another.

00:02:29.419 --> 00:02:31.609
That has a practical consequence.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:36.518
A domain's share of stories tends to
follow its share of approved prompts.

00:02:36.518 --> 00:02:40.765
And a prompt that people often
swap away from collects very few.

00:02:40.765 --> 00:02:46.139
So balance the pool: aim for the same
number of approved prompts in every domain,

00:02:46.139 --> 00:02:50.717
and when one domain falls behind,
add a prompt there, not everywhere.

00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:55.768
The prompt library has thirty-two
ready-made prompts per domain,

00:02:55.768 --> 00:03:00.728
and you can filter them by Culture
Mosaic tile, by topic, or by words.

00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:03.491
Library prompts go in already approved,

00:03:03.491 --> 00:03:06.679
so read them in your
client's language first.

00:03:06.679 --> 00:03:11.072
Northwind changed "customer or
client" to "patient or family".

00:03:11.072 --> 00:03:16.457
If a scored Culture Mosaic is linked,
leads also see its three lowest tiles,

00:03:16.457 --> 00:03:18.937
with prompts on those listed first.

00:03:18.937 --> 00:03:23.896
For Northwind, that's Collaboration,
Change Ready and Empowered Teams.

00:03:23.896 --> 00:03:28.077
Treat them as claims the stories
can confirm or contradict,

00:03:28.077 --> 00:03:32.611
not as the problem to find, and
keep prompts on other areas too.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.304
After their story, respondents answer
the follow-up questions: the triangle,

00:03:38.304 --> 00:03:40.153
two sliders and the stones.

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:43.646
The product's own advice
is that they already work,

00:03:43.646 --> 00:03:47.480
and running them as they are
is a perfectly good choice.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.809
If you do edit them, two cautions.

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:54.260
Rewording or swapping a slider's
ends, or the triangle's corners,

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:59.943
doesn't change which end counts as healthier,
or which corner counts as concerning.

00:03:59.943 --> 00:04:02.888
Those are separate
settings, so check them.

00:04:02.888 --> 00:04:05.901
And keep the wording
identical across waves,

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:08.709
or the comparison stops meaning anything.

00:04:08.709 --> 00:04:11.448
Make any edits before the link goes out.

00:04:11.933 --> 00:04:15.422
A dig hears only from the
people the link reaches,

00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:18.004
so plan distribution like a campaign.

00:04:18.004 --> 00:04:20.656
Northwind's CEO announced it by email.

00:04:20.656 --> 00:04:25.890
The link and a QR code went up at every
daily huddle, for the whole window,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.798
because many frontline staff
never open all-staff email.

00:04:29.798 --> 00:04:33.008
Managers read a prompt
aloud at team meetings,

00:04:33.008 --> 00:04:36.008
and there was one
reminder at the midpoint.

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:39.986
Promise only what the page
and your distribution provide.

00:04:39.986 --> 00:04:42.986
The page says it takes
about three minutes,

00:04:42.986 --> 00:04:46.127
that stories are read
by AI to find patterns,

00:04:46.127 --> 00:04:49.895
and asks people to write
about what happened, not who.

00:04:49.895 --> 00:04:54.849
It asks where someone works only if
you listed sites before collection,

00:04:54.849 --> 00:04:56.803
and that answer is optional.

00:04:56.803 --> 00:05:01.618
Without it, you'll know a story's
domain, but not where it came from.

00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:07.908
May the third, one week into collection,
and a hundred and three stories are in.

00:05:07.908 --> 00:05:10.362
Six domains are in double figures.

00:05:10.362 --> 00:05:13.970
Artifacts has two, from a
single, abstract prompt.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.919
Stories close in two weeks.

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:17.363
What should Alex do?

00:05:17.867 --> 00:05:21.313
On the close date, Northwind
pressed Close collection,

00:05:21.313 --> 00:05:24.121
which stops new stories
and can be reversed.

00:05:24.121 --> 00:05:26.546
They didn't mark the program complete,

00:05:26.546 --> 00:05:29.482
because wave two will
run in the same program.

00:05:29.482 --> 00:05:34.333
Before you close, check that each domain
has at least ten stories behind its

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:38.098
thinnest input, or that you've
accepted it won't be scored,

00:05:38.098 --> 00:05:40.332
and close on the day you announced.

00:05:40.833 --> 00:05:44.778
Time for the module check:
six questions, five to pass.

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:48.811
Here's your Story Prompt
Review Card: the five tests,

00:05:48.811 --> 00:05:53.894
and checklists for before the link opens,
while stories arrive, and closing.

00:05:53.894 --> 00:05:54.697
Download it.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:55.964
To sum up.

00:05:55.964 --> 00:05:57.950
Teams first, then prompts.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:03.528
Every prompt asks for one thing that
happened, fits any role, stays open,

00:06:03.528 --> 00:06:06.584
states no finding, and points at nobody.

00:06:06.584 --> 00:06:10.022
Prompts are served at
random across the pool,

00:06:10.022 --> 00:06:13.460
so balance it and act
early on a thin domain.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.593
The follow-up questions work as they are.

00:06:16.593 --> 00:06:20.184
And the link reaches only
the people you reach.

00:06:20.184 --> 00:06:22.629
Next: from evidence to findings.
